      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Oakville boasts several historic landmarks older than fifty years, preserving the town’s early architectural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://aboutstlouis.com/local/communities/oakville-missouri",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Eugene Nims House, also known as Bee Tree Farm, was built in 1929 and now serves as a local historic landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://aboutstlouis.com/local/communities/oakville-missouri",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Oakville never incorporated and has no city hall, a unique feature among nearby communities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.regionalvoices.org/oakville-missouris-history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town sits where the Meramec and Mississippi Rivers meet, a spot settled before the 1804 Louisiana Purchase.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
